ws are split into top, center (unpinned), and bottom pinned rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enable-row-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enable Row Pinning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To use row pinning, add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPinningFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" to your features. Row pinning does not require a row model factory."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  rowPinningFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPinningFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"row-pinning-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Pinning State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" state stores row IDs in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":" arrays:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type RowPinningState = {\n  top: string[]\n  bottom: string[]\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pin rows by default with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.rowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    rowPinning: {\n      top: ['0'],\n      bottom: ['3'],\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to manage row pinning outside of the table instance, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the pinning state without coupling that code to the table instance."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/svelte-store'\nimport type { RowPinningState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst rowPinningAtom = createAtom<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst rowPinning = useSelector(rowPinningAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data\n  },\n  atoms: {\n    rowPinning: rowPinningAtom,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.rowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRowPinningChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createTableState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst [rowPinning, setRowPinning] = createTableState<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get rowPinning() {\n      return rowPinning()\n    },\n  },\n  onRowPinningChange: setRowPinning,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setRowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" to update the state directly, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetRowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.rowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetRowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" to clear both pinned row arrays."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.setRowPinning({\n  top: ['0', '2'],\n  bottom: ['8'],\n})\n\ntable.resetRowPinning()\ntable.resetRowPinning(true)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pin-rows-with-row-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pin Rows With Row APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each row exposes APIs for checking whether it can be pinned, reading its pinned position, and changing its pinned position."}]},{"type":"code","value":"row.getCanPin()\nrow.getIsPinned() // 'top', 'bottom', or false\nrow.getPinnedIndex()\n\nrow.pin('top')\nrow.pin('bottom')\nrow.pin(false)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can use these APIs to build pinning controls:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"{#if row.getCanPin()}\n  <div>\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('top')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'top'}>Top</button\n    >\n    <button onclick={() => row.pin(false)} disabled={!row.getIsPinned()}\n      >Center</button\n    >\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('bottom')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'bottom'}>Bottom</button\n    >\n  </div>\n{/if}","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.pin"},{"type":"text","value":" API also accepts "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeLeafRows"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeParentRows"},{"type":"text","value":" flags. These can be useful when pinning grouped or expanded rows and deciding whether related parent or leaf rows should move with the row."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"row-pinning-table-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Pinning Table APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row pinning splits the current row model into 3 row lists:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.getTopRows()\ntable.getCenterRows()\ntable.getBottomRows()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you render pinned rows in separate table sections, use those APIs directly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<tbody>\n  {#each table.getTopRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getCenterRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <TableRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getBottomRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n</tbody>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getIsSomeRowsPinned()"},{"type":"text","value":" to check whether any rows are pinned, or pass a position to check a specific pinned region."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.getIsSomeRowsPinned()\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('top')\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('bottom')","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"disable-row-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable Row Pinning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, all rows can be pinned. You can disable row pinning for the whole table or decide per row with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableRowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableRowPinning: (row) => row.original.status !== 'archived',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"keep-pinned-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep Pinned Rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepPinnedRows"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", so pinned rows stay visible in their pinned region even when they would otherwise be filtered or paginated out of the center rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keepPinnedRows"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" if pinned rows should only render when they are present in the current filtered and paginated row model."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  keepPinnedRows: false,\n})","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"row-pinning-setup","text":"Row Pinning Setup","level":3},{"id":"row-pinning-svelte-guide","text":"Row Pinning (Svelte) Guide","level":2},{"id":"enable-row-pinning","text":"Enable Row Pinning","level":3},{"id":"row-pinning-state","text":"Row Pinning State","level":3},{"id":"pin-rows-with-row-apis","text":"Pin Rows With Row APIs","level":3},{"id":"row-pinning-table-apis","text":"Row Pinning Table APIs","level":3},{"id":"disable-row-pinning","text":"Disable Row Pinning","level":3},{"id":"keep-pinned-rows","text":"Keep Pinned Rows","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Svelte examples:\n\n- [Row Pinning](../examples/row-pinning)\n\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Svelte state to `createTable`.\n\n### Row Pinning Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use row pinning features. Adding the row pinning feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPinningFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPinningFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Row Pinning (Svelte) Guide\n\nRow pinning lets you keep selected rows in top or bottom row regions while the rest of the rows render in the center region.\n\nThere are 2 table features that can reorder rows, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. **Row Pinning** - If pinning, rows are split into top, center (unpinned), and bottom pinned rows.\n2. [Sorting](./sorting)\n\n### Enable Row Pinning\n\nTo use row pinning, add `rowPinningFeature` to your features. Row pinning does not require a row model factory.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  rowPinningFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPinningFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Row Pinning State\n\nThe `rowPinning` state stores row IDs in `top` and `bottom` arrays:\n\n```ts\ntype RowPinningState = {\n  top: string[]\n  bottom: string[]\n}\n```\n\nYou can pin rows by default with `initialState.rowPinning`:\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    rowPinning: {\n      top: ['0'],\n      bottom: ['3'],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIf you need to manage row pinning outside of the table instance, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the pinning state without coupling that code to the table instance.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/svelte-store'\nimport type { RowPinningState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst rowPinningAtom = createAtom<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst rowPinning = useSelector(rowPinningAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data\n  },\n  atoms: {\n    rowPinning: rowPinningAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.rowPinning` plus `onRowPinningChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nimport { createTableState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst [rowPinning, setRowPinning] = createTableState<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get rowPinning() {\n      return rowPinning()\n    },\n  },\n  onRowPinningChange: setRowPinning,\n})\n```\n\nUse `table.setRowPinning` to update the state directly, and `table.resetRowPinning` to reset it to `initialState.rowPinning`. Pass `true` to `resetRowPinning` to clear both pinned row arrays.\n\n```ts\ntable.setRowPinning({\n  top: ['0', '2'],\n  bottom: ['8'],\n})\n\ntable.resetRowPinning()\ntable.resetRowPinning(true)\n```\n\n### Pin Rows With Row APIs\n\nEach row exposes APIs for checking whether it can be pinned, reading its pinned position, and changing its pinned position.\n\n```ts\nrow.getCanPin()\nrow.getIsPinned() // 'top', 'bottom', or false\nrow.getPinnedIndex()\n\nrow.pin('top')\nrow.pin('bottom')\nrow.pin(false)\n```\n\nYou can use these APIs to build pinning controls:\n\n```svelte\n{#if row.getCanPin()}\n  <div>\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('top')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'top'}>Top</button\n    >\n    <button onclick={() => row.pin(false)} disabled={!row.getIsPinned()}\n      >Center</button\n    >\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('bottom')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'bottom'}>Bottom</button\n    >\n  </div>\n{/if}\n```\n\nThe `row.pin` API also accepts `includeLeafRows` and `includeParentRows` flags. These can be useful when pinning grouped or expanded rows and deciding whether related parent or leaf rows should move with the row.\n\n### Row Pinning Table APIs\n\nRow pinning splits the current row model into 3 row lists:\n\n```ts\ntable.getTopRows()\ntable.getCenterRows()\ntable.getBottomRows()\n```\n\nIf you render pinned rows in separate table sections, use those APIs directly:\n\n```svelte\n<tbody>\n  {#each table.getTopRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getCenterRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <TableRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getBottomRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n</tbody>\n```\n\nUse `table.getIsSomeRowsPinned()` to check whether any rows are pinned, or pass a position to check a specific pinned region.\n\n```ts\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned()\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('top')\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('bottom')\n```\n\n### Disable Row Pinning\n\nBy default, all rows can be pinned. You can disable row pinning for the whole table or decide per row with `enableRowPinning`.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableRowPinning: (row) => row.original.status !== 'archived',\n})\n```\n\n### Keep Pinned Rows\n\nBy default, `keepPinnedRows` is `true`, so pinned rows stay visible in their pinned region even when they would otherwise be filtered or paginated out of the center rows.\n\nSet `keepPinnedRows` to `false` if pinned rows should only render when they are present in the current filtered and paginated row model.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  keepPinnedRows: false,\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Row Pinning (Svelte) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Svelte examples:\n\n- [Row Pinning](../examples/row-pinning)\n\nUse getters for reactive inputs such as `data` when passing Svelte state to `createTable`.\n\n### Row Pinning Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use row pinning features. Adding the row pinning feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowPinningFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPinningFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Row Pinning (Svelte) Guide\n\nRow pinning lets you keep selected rows in top or bottom row regions while the rest of the rows render in the center region.\n\nThere are 2 table features that can reorder rows, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. **Row Pinning** - If pinning, rows are split into top, center (unpinned), and bottom pinned rows.\n2. [Sorting](./sorting)\n\n### Enable Row Pinning\n\nTo use row pinning, add `rowPinningFeature` to your features. Row pinning does not require a row model factory.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  rowPinningFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  createTable,\n} from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowPinningFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Row Pinning State\n\nThe `rowPinning` state stores row IDs in `top` and `bottom` arrays:\n\n```ts\ntype RowPinningState = {\n  top: string[]\n  bottom: string[]\n}\n```\n\nYou can pin rows by default with `initialState.rowPinning`:\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    rowPinning: {\n      top: ['0'],\n      bottom: ['3'],\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIf you need to manage row pinning outside of the table instance, the recommended v9 approach is an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the pinning state without coupling that code to the table instance.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/svelte-store'\nimport type { RowPinningState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst rowPinningAtom = createAtom<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst rowPinning = useSelector(rowPinningAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return data\n  },\n  atoms: {\n    rowPinning: rowPinningAtom,\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.rowPinning` plus `onRowPinningChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nimport { createTableState } from '@tanstack/svelte-table'\n\nconst [rowPinning, setRowPinning] = createTableState<RowPinningState>({\n  top: [],\n  bottom: [],\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    get rowPinning() {\n      return rowPinning()\n    },\n  },\n  onRowPinningChange: setRowPinning,\n})\n```\n\nUse `table.setRowPinning` to update the state directly, and `table.resetRowPinning` to reset it to `initialState.rowPinning`. Pass `true` to `resetRowPinning` to clear both pinned row arrays.\n\n```ts\ntable.setRowPinning({\n  top: ['0', '2'],\n  bottom: ['8'],\n})\n\ntable.resetRowPinning()\ntable.resetRowPinning(true)\n```\n\n### Pin Rows With Row APIs\n\nEach row exposes APIs for checking whether it can be pinned, reading its pinned position, and changing its pinned position.\n\n```ts\nrow.getCanPin()\nrow.getIsPinned() // 'top', 'bottom', or false\nrow.getPinnedIndex()\n\nrow.pin('top')\nrow.pin('bottom')\nrow.pin(false)\n```\n\nYou can use these APIs to build pinning controls:\n\n```svelte\n{#if row.getCanPin()}\n  <div>\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('top')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'top'}>Top</button\n    >\n    <button onclick={() => row.pin(false)} disabled={!row.getIsPinned()}\n      >Center</button\n    >\n    <button\n      onclick={() => row.pin('bottom')}\n      disabled={row.getIsPinned() === 'bottom'}>Bottom</button\n    >\n  </div>\n{/if}\n```\n\nThe `row.pin` API also accepts `includeLeafRows` and `includeParentRows` flags. These can be useful when pinning grouped or expanded rows and deciding whether related parent or leaf rows should move with the row.\n\n### Row Pinning Table APIs\n\nRow pinning splits the current row model into 3 row lists:\n\n```ts\ntable.getTopRows()\ntable.getCenterRows()\ntable.getBottomRows()\n```\n\nIf you render pinned rows in separate table sections, use those APIs directly:\n\n```svelte\n<tbody>\n  {#each table.getTopRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getCenterRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <TableRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n  {#each table.getBottomRows() as row (row.id)}\n    <PinnedRow {row} />\n  {/each}\n</tbody>\n```\n\nUse `table.getIsSomeRowsPinned()` to check whether any rows are pinned, or pass a position to check a specific pinned region.\n\n```ts\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned()\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('top')\ntable.getIsSomeRowsPinned('bottom')\n```\n\n### Disable Row Pinning\n\nBy default, all rows can be pinned. You can disable row pinning for the whole table or decide per row with `enableRowPinning`.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  enableRowPinning: (row) => row.original.status !== 'archived',\n})\n```\n\n### Keep Pinned Rows\n\nBy default, `keepPinnedRows` is `true`, so pinned rows stay visible in their pinned region even when they would otherwise be filtered or paginated out of the center rows.\n\nSet `keepPinnedRows` to `false` if pinned rows should only render when they are present in the current filtered and paginated row model.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  keepPinnedRows: false,\n})\n```\n"}
